---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="How we write the Taxfold guides"
  description="How the Taxfold guides are drafted with AI assistance, fact-checked and reviewed by a person before publication, and corrected."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-6">How we write the guides</h1>

    <p>
      Every guide on taxfold.app is signed by the <strong>Taxfold team</strong> at Opal Harmony P.C., the company that
      makes the app (<a href="/about/">about us</a>). This page explains how a guide gets from a question to a published
      page, because you should know how what you are reading was made.
    </p>

    <h2>AI assistance, stated plainly</h2>
    <p>
      We use AI writing tools. A guide starts as a brief from us: the question it answers, the sources it must rely on
      and what it must not claim. An AI model drafts the page from that brief. We do not publish drafts as they come
      out.
    </p>

    <h2>Checked before it is published</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Fact check.</strong> A separate AI review pass checks every claim, number, date and link in the draft
        against its sources and lists what it could not confirm. Its findings are posted on the change for us to read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Human review.</strong> A person on the team reads the page, the fact-check findings and the page's
        title, description and structured data, and fixes or cuts anything that does not hold. Nothing publishes
        until that person approves it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public record.</strong> The site's source is public on
        <a href="https://github.com/getsinew/taxfold.app">GitHub</a>. Each guide arrives as a pull request, and its
        fact-check comments and the approval that published it stay visible there.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What the guides are, and are not</h2>
    <p>
      The guides are general information about keeping and organising receipts and expense records. They are not
      tax, legal or accounting advice, and they cannot account for your situation. Where a rule depends on the
      country, the guide names the country and links the official source. Check anything you rely on with your tax
      authority or an accountant.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some guides mention Taxfold where it is relevant to the task. We make the app, so read those mentions as
      coming from its maker.
    </p>

    <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      When a guide changes in a way that matters, its "Last updated" date changes with it. If you find a mistake,
      write to <a href="mailto:hello@taxfold.app">hello@taxfold.app</a>; we correct errors and do not hide that a page
      was changed.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
